"""
# Chained substring().to_int() Specification

**Feature IDs:** N/A
**Category:** Runtime
**Difficulty:** 2/5
**Status:** Fixed
**Requirements:** N/A
**Plan:** N/A
**Design:** N/A
**Research:** doc/08_tracking/bug/jit_substring_chained_to_int_returns_pointer_2026-08-04.md

## Overview

`jit_substring_chained_to_int_returns_pointer_2026-08-04` reported that

```simple
arg.substring(10).to_int()
```

returned the raw **text pointer** under the JIT, silently, while binding the
substring to a `val` first and then calling `.to_int()` on it gave the right
answer. That asymmetry is the whole defect, so this spec asserts the **chained**
form and keeps the `val`-bound form only as an attribution control — a guard
written the `val`-bound way would be vacuous here.

The failure was silent and produced a large plausible-looking integer, so the
guard asserts an exact value and additionally pins that the chained and
`val`-bound forms agree with each other.

Re-checked 2026-09-12: chained and bound forms agree on both lanes.

## Syntax

```simple
val arg: text = "--timeout=800"
arg.substring(10).to_int() ?? -1     # must be 800
```

"""

use std.spec


fn flag_value() -> text:
    return "--timeout=800"


# ============================================================================
# Test Group 1: the record's own reproducer
# ============================================================================

describe "chained substring().to_int()":
    """
    ## `--timeout=800`

    The exact reproducer. The defect returned a pointer-valued integer.
    """

    it "parses the chained form to the right integer":
        val arg: text = flag_value()
        expect(arg.substring(10).to_int() ?? 0 - 1).to_equal(800)

    it "yields the expected substring text":
        val arg: text = flag_value()
        expect(arg.substring(10)).to_equal("800")

    it "yields the expected substring length":
        val arg: text = flag_value()
        expect(arg.substring(10).len()).to_equal(3)


# ============================================================================
# Test Group 2: chained vs val-bound agreement
# ============================================================================

describe "chained and val-bound forms agree":
    """
    ## Attribution control

    The `val`-bound form was correct while the defect was live. Asserting that
    the two forms agree is what makes a future divergence attributable to the
    chaining rather than to parsing.
    """

    it "matches the val-bound result":
        val arg: text = flag_value()
        val s: text = arg.substring(10)
        expect(arg.substring(10).to_int() ?? 0 - 1).to_equal(s.to_int() ?? 0 - 2)

    it "chains over a two-argument substring too":
        val arg: text = "abc123def"
        expect(arg.substring(3, 6).to_int() ?? 0 - 1).to_equal(123)

    it "chains to_i64 as well as to_int":
        val arg: text = flag_value()
        expect(arg.substring(10).to_i64() ?? 0 - 1).to_equal(800)
